{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful and ambitious ruler who navigated complex political landscapes and faced constant threats from Muslim forces and rival European powers. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering aimed at consolidating his control and expanding his territory.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were spent dealing with internal challenges including rebellions from local lords and external pressures from the Zengid dynasty rulers of Syria. He quickly established his authority through a combination of military strength and shrewd political alliances. His marriage to Countess Melisende of Jerusalem a daughter of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem significantly strengthened his position and solidified the ties between Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. This union was strategically important providing crucial military and financial support for Tripoli's defense.\n\nRaymond III engaged in numerous military campaigns throughout his reign. He fought against Saladin and his successors in several battles attempting to maintain Christian control over territories in the Levant. While he experienced both victories and defeats his military efforts were largely successful in preserving Tripoli's independence. He also focused on strengthening Tripoli's fortifications and developing its economy.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nA key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his diplomatic skill. He skillfully cultivated alliances with various powers including the Kingdom of Jerusalem the Holy Roman Empire and other Crusader states. He understood the importance of maintaining a balance of power in the region and used diplomacy to prevent being isolated. He often played rivalries between Muslim rulers to his advantage securing favorable terms for Tripoli. His relationship with Saladin was complex characterized by periods of warfare and negotiation. Despite their frequent clashes Raymond III occasionally engaged in diplomatic exchanges with Saladin demonstrating his pragmatism.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III's reign was a period of relative stability and prosperity for the County of Tripoli. He left behind a well-defended and economically vibrant territory. His descendants continued to rule Tripoli for several generations. He is remembered as a capable and resourceful ruler who successfully defended Tripoli against numerous threats and maintained its independence for many years. His diplomatic achievements and military campaigns solidified Tripoli's position as a significant power in the Levant during the Crusades. He died in 1199 and was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. His death marked the end of an era and the beginning of a period of increasing instability for the County of Tripoli.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0011152960360050201}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2664777338504791}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.12502680718898773}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.08393791317939758}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00027021096320822835}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.3841574147809297e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -6.318072337307967e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.2755313036905136e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0014255610294640064}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -0.02121167443692